te_shrhldr_yield":0.10088072399093619,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":100.0,"_gate_share_shrink":0.047835492701197024,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":null,"_gp_sbc_dilution":null,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.00458,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":3.1,"_gate_insider_buying":1.0,"_gp_insider_buying":true,"_score_insider_buying":100.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Sixth Street Specialty Lending, Inc. (NYSE: TSLX) is a business development company, private equity, loan and mezzanine fund. The fund provides senior secured loans (first-lien, second-lien, and unitranche), unsecured loans, mezzanine debt, and investments in corporate bonds and equity securities and structured products, non-control structured equity, and common equity with a focus on co-investments for organic growth, acquisitions, market or product expansion, restructuring initiatives, recapitalizations, growth capital, buyout, and refinancing. The fund invests in business services, software & technology, healthcare, energy, consumer & retail, manufacturing, internet services, consumer products, hotel, gaming, leisure, transportation, human resource support services, oil, gas, consumable fuels, pharmaceuticals, industrials, royalty related businesses, education, and specialty finance. The fund seeks to finance and lending to middle market companies principally located in the United States. The fund invests in companies with enterprise value between $50 million and $1000 million or more and EBITDA between $10 million and $250 million and equity investment between $15 million and $100 million. The debt transaction size is between $15 million and $350 million. The fund invests across the spectrum of the capital structure and can arrange syndicated transactions of up to $500 million and hold sizeable positions within its credits.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Robert J. Stanley | CEO & Director | Age 51","financial_summary":["Sixth Street Specialty Lending, is a small-cap financial services business with a $1.7B market cap.","Returns on capital run at 11% against a 7% cost of capital, a 3-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","Management returns a meaningful 10.1% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.6) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio looks stretched \u2014 the dividend\u2019s own margin of safety is thin","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 35% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Large open-market insider purchases (net $11.2M) \u2014 management putting its own capital behind the business","11 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"What Sixth Street Specialty Lending (TSLX) Said on Its Q3 Earnings Call","source":"finance.yahoo.com","